I'd never ascended a Monk and, given Sporkhack's particularities (NOTE
-- this post will be SPOILY), it seemed like a good choice. In
Sporkhack, eating a corpse that could give you resistance in vanilla
will give you resistance, but only a percentage. So, eating a
homunculus would give one 5% poison resistance. The Monk, however, as
she levels up, gets full resistances.

This system also makes crowning a much better thing than in vanilla.

Anyway, I headed down toward Sokoban, hoping to find reflection there,
and picked up a horse (Petey) along the way to go along with my cat
(Fluffy). Happened upon a very large hardware store, which gave me a
lockpick, a magic whistle, a bag of tricks and pickaxe. The next level
had an arch-lich running a scroll and spellbook shop. In Sporkhack,
identify scrolls self-identify, so I made sure to pick up all of these
(three or four, I believe) so that I could mass bless them once I got
my hands on some holy water. Sokoban presented no problems, though, as
usual, the different orientation of the rooms made my head hurt a bit.
Still, managed to get the bag of holding (dang) without hosing my
luck.

The mines were next on the menu and, when I came to Minetown, I found
the place had been ransacked by orcs -- the town was barricaded and
watchmen's bodies littered the corridors. Once I dug my way in, I
found the shopkeepers' bodies and a desecrated temple, converted to
Moloch. This is Orctown.

It has its advantages. The altar can be safely converted, for example,
so long as you can handle the demon that Moloch is almost sure to send
(yes, you have to be careful converting altars -- the gods in
Sporkhack often send along messengers to convey their profound
displeasure when you take away one of their altars). Plus, there are
no watchmen to be bothered when you use the fountains to dilute
potions. The lack of a priest, however, means that one may have to
wait until the Valley of the Dead to buy protection.

The orcs had carried off most of the shopkeepers' plunder, but they
did leave candles lying about, and I found wands of striking and fire
on the poor shopkeepers' bodies. .

In any case, I killed the orcs, converted the altar, killed the herzou
(I think?) that Moloch sent with a wand of cold, and set about
sacrificing to make holy water. I mass blessed my ID scrolls and set
about ID'ing my inventory, which had a ring of free action from
Sokoban! That done, I delved to the bottom.

On the way down, I was very careful to search often. In fact, until
Mine's End, I kept my robe off, not wanting to see it destroyed by a
polymorph trap. Luckily, I was able to find several and avoid them.
Mine's End was the King's Wine Cellar, so I stocked up on booze and
grabbed a luckstone -- my second, as I'd also found one on the mine
floor a few levels above.

As I entered level 14, I found myself surrounded by a black pudding, a
horse and a rock troll. I'd found an enchanted Dwarven Helm (+1) and
had dwarven iron shoes from the mines, but, still, my AC was only
somewhere around zero. I'd had plenty of experience with the kinds of
heavy damage a rock troll can do to an unprepared character, so I
didn't plan to engage it in melee. Instead, I'd put it to sleep, as
I'd acquired the sleep spell from the Arch-Lich's store.

The beam reflected back at me from its amulet of reflection. And, I
quickly discovered, it was weilding a unicorn horn -- 1/3 of my HP
evaporated. It was then that I noticed the ghost in the corne. This
was a bones level.

Sporkhack often put you on the square next to the staircase when you
ascend or descend, which was the case here, so going back upstairs
wasn't an option. I couldn't run because I had monsters or walls on
all sides. I didn't have a wand or scroll of teleportation, which left
one option. I dug a hole with a wand of digging through the floor.

We landed in a mid-sized room, and the rock troll had followed, which
I expected, but I figured I was probably faster than him (thank
goodness for monks' starting with "fast"). A couple of turns later, a
square separated the two of us. I dug down again and was blissfully
alone.

By this point, I had an amulet of ESP, so my plan was to ascend back
up to the bones pile without encountering the troll, rummage through
the loot and, I hoped, find something that could help me kill the
troll. Which, indeed, was the case. Because the room had not one, but
two bones piles, and both of the characters had been killed by the
very same rock troll. One of them had even burned "Elbereth Elbereth
Elbereth" on the upstair, perhaps not realizing that Elbereth is by no
means foolproof in Sporkhack and is largely useless against powerful
monsters. But what a bonanza! I found gauntlets of force!

For a monk in Sporkhack, finding gauntlets of force is like a lawful
archeologist finding a fully enchanted Grayswandir. First, so far as I
can tell, they stun anything you hit, which greatly reduces a
monster's ability to fight back. Second, depending on the size of the
monster, they push it backwards. Man-sized creatures like mind flayers
can go flying five or six squares, and even a creature like a
Jabberwock can be pushed backwards one square. Dragons and giants
don't move, but they are stunned.

But there's more! A magic lamp, a backup robe, a ring of levitation, a
magic harp!  A scroll of enchant armor! A spellbook of obtain food! (a
great change -- at Basic or better, it creates a food ration or two at
your feet. Very useful)

 I enchanted the gauntlets of force up to +2 and converted the lamp
into a pair of blessed fireproof +2 speed boots. I was ready for the
troll.

First, I cleared out a throne room and a beehive -- apparently, these
two characters hadn't gotten far beyond the upstair, made a stash out
the throne room chest and headed downwards to meet the troll.

Unfortunately, two can play the digging game, and once the rock troll
felt the bite of my gauntlets, it dug downward, too. I chased it
downward for three more levels before I finally vanquished it and took
the amulet of reflection. Found an altar, converted it and ... got
crowned. Crap. The best I got out of my god was a +3 Dwarven Spear.

Medusa's Isle was very different -- mostly water and packed with
Ravens. I had to head back upstairs a couple of times to heal before I
was able to take them all. Medusa went down quickly thanks to the
amulet. The next level was a taste of Gehennom.

Gehennom is VERY different in Sporkhack. Mine-like caverns surrounded
by lava, with lava rivers sometimes bisecting them. I can't imagine
doing it without a ring of levitation. Even fireproofed water walking
boots are dangerous, since powerful spellcasting monsters can remove
your foo-proofing.

It's a lot of fun.

On that level, I managed to charm a purple worm, which I named
"Mouthy." My other pets had disappeared or gotten themselves killed,
much to my disappointment. I always get attached to my early pets.
Mouthy was a huge help in the castle, as I didn't have magic
resistance and was pretty worried about the master lich that was
there. I used the backdoor entrance and placed Mouthy in front. He
took care of business. I did end up in melee with the weakened Master
Lich, but luckily got in the first blow and stunned him. A few more
pummels and it disintegrated.

I had a lot of gold, so I thought that, while I was there, I may as
well visit the Valley of the Dead and buy some protection. With
Mouthy, it wasn't a problem, especially since the only lich was a
plain old lich. Making sure Mouthy was in a different part of the
valley chomping on corpses, I bought protection from the priestess of
Moloch, which made me kind of queasy, but sometimes you've got to
break a few eggs to make an omlette, right? Polero was not above
eating floating eyes, wraith corpses, mind flayers or black dragons in
the name of his higher goal.

I headed back up to my stash and made my first mistake. The wand was
(0:1). I had plenty of blanked scrolls, so I wished for a magic
marker, which aren't randomly generated at all in Sporkhack, so far as
I know. I set out to write a blessed scroll of charging and got: "You
don't know how to write that!" Auuugggghghhh!

Well, couldn't be helped. I'd have to hope I'd find one. I used the
rest of the charges in the magic marker to write enchant armor scrolls
and mostly bring my armor up to maximum.

I finally felt ready for Master Kaen. I was a bit worried about how
I'd manage Mouthy around my quest leader, since he was now level 27 or
something crazy like that, but he ran afoul of a polymorph trap on the
first level fo the Quest and turned into a ghoul. Oh well.

Surprisingly, Master Kaen was, quite literally, a pushover. I punched
him a couple of times until he fell back into lava, and that was all
she wrote. My wand of cold retrieved the Bell and a pickaxe finished
the job to get the Eyes of the Overworld. And then, I forgot to put my
pickaxe back in my bag and ruined my chance of never hitting with a
wielded weapon. Oh well.

Immediately after exiting the quest, I tamed a horse and played a bit
of polymorph trap hopscotch. I got a lemure. Fantastic.

But wait! The lemure intentionally jumped into the polymorph trap and
transformed into a Balrog! Now we're talking! Barry picked up a double
headed axe soI thought it might be time to pay a visit to the
shopkeepers upstairs. And, luckily enough, in the (now deceased) arch-
lich's shop, I found a scroll named THARR that, yes, was a scroll of
charging. My last four wishes were for a blessed fireproof +2 t-shirt,
a blessed rustproof +2 helm of telepathy, a spellbook of magic missile
and another magic marker. Unfortunately, Barry got lost on the way
down. I found him again on the way back up, but he was feral, so I had
to kill him. Too bad.

I got lucky in Gehennom. Magic eyes and liches didn't catch me in the
open, where the lich could surround me with nasties, curse my stuff
and degrade my armor while the magic eye slowed me down to a crawl and
blew up any healing potions I dared take out with a flaming gaze.
Likewise, Nazguls and their spammy stunning scream were usually within
close range, so I could take them down relatively quickly. I had a
suit of yellow dragon scales just in case, but it's so hard to hit
anything wearing armor as a monk, I never resorted to them.

 Asmodeus has nearly killed earlier characters with his psi bolt from
hell (hint: wear a tinfoil hat), but he was no problem at all with the
gauntlets of force. For Orcus, I'd accidentally teleported one level
below Orcus town after a quick trip to my stash to re-learn some
spells, but it was all to the good -- I emerged right next to him from
the stairwell and, thanks to the gauntlets, he never even got a shot
off of his wand. I found another a bit later just lying on the dungeon
floor.

Vlad took me down about five levels with Lifestealer, but that was
easily remedied through alchemy. I dropped that evil blade into the
lava -- I didn't want there to be any chance of something else
weilding that monstrosity against me.

Rodney fell to Orcus' wand of death and the Sanctum posed few
problems, as I was very careful to block off the passage behind me
with a scroll of earth (simply teeming with nasties thanks to a master
lich and a nalfashnee and picked off magic eyes, Nalfashnees and
priests with magic missile or, in extreme cases, the WoD. The high
priest of Moloch also fell to a WoD. I grabbed the AoY, quaffed a
cursed potion of gain level and started up.

By the way, I encountered a Long Worm near a polymorph trap with 421
HP. Is that normal? It wasn't a pet of mine, as I name them all and
this beast was nameless.

Sporkhack has no mysterious force, thank goodness. But it does have
monsters waiting at each upstair. Alas, just above the castle, a mind
flayer successfully sucked my brain. Nothing the restore ability spell
couldn't handle (unicorn horns won't do the trick in Spork), but it
was a serous pain finding the upstairs. The helm of telepathy came in
handy -- I just looked for concentrations of monsters.

A soldier donated a third WoD, so I was well stocked by the time I
reached the planes, which posed no problems. I didn't use conflict,
since it's nerfed. Even air didn't pose a serious threat -- if I got
engulfed, the gauntlets stunned the elemental so that it couldn't harm
me very much.

On the astral plane, I had peace and quiet -- initially at least. So I
took out my cursed scroll of genocide, summoned eight purple worms
and, with my magic harp, tamed them all! Me and my purple worm army
rolled over everything in sight. It was beautiful. I still had to
quaff 6 potions of full healing, but truly was never in any danger.
Even with reflection and magic resistance, those magic missiles hurt!
I lost my last character on the Astral Plane (forgot to put on a ring
of free action and got paralyzed), so I wanted to make sure it went
well this time around.

The second altar was mine. And now, I'm taking a long break from
Nethack. Too addictive, and I need to spend more time focused on other
things.

Derek -- what a great variant! I love it!

A few suggestions:

1) With gauntlets of Force, Kaen needs to be beefed up a bit. Perhaps
stun resistance and the ability to cast "Fly"?

2) As far as I know, there's no way to encounter a magic marker in the
game without wishing. Seems there should be at least one. How about
giving the Wizard of Yendor a magic marker? That's late enough in the
game to where it won't totally unbalance a character. Otherwise, a
newbie really has no way of knowing they even exist unless they're
spoiled.


